I think we've only just begun the  adjustment process and growth rates are  going to slow down a lot more my  expectation is that under President Xi's  term roughly from two 2013-2023 average  growth rates are unlikely to exceed  three to four percent and that's not my  prediction that's the upper limit of my  prediction I really don't think China's  going to be able to grow much faster  than that and that may seem like a very  surprising statement and certainly a few  years ago it was considered even more  surprising but there there are two sets  of reasons why I think that's likely to  be the case the first is very  straightforward it's just the historical  precedence we've had depending on how  you define a growth miracle we've had  perhaps 30 to 40 of them since the  Second World War i define it as more  than 10 years of more than seven percent  growth and they all follow very similar  patterns is very little variation in the  early stages these countries tend to  have very low level levels of investment  relative to their absorption capacity so  in that case the best strategy is to  increase investment as rapidly as  possible and that's what they all do but  are undeveloped countries the difference  between a China and the UK is not that  the UK has more investment it's probably  institutional it's probably has  something to do with the financial  system the legal system all of these  other issues so rich countries are able  to absorb higher levels of capital more  productively almost by definition a poor  country can't do that so a country like  China with very high levels of  investment the highest ever recorded and  very high growth rates of investment it  was always likely to reach a point at  which it was no longer able to absorb it  productively and I believe we reach that  point quite a long time ago most people  agree that we've passed that point the  debate is is how long and once that  happens almost by definition get is  growing faster than debt servicing  capacity and when that happens of course  that means debt is growing unsustainable  so it's probably not a surprise under  those conditions that every single  growth miracle certainly since world war  two and probably before has ended either  in a debt crisis or in a lost decade or  two lost decades in the case of Japan of  a very very low growth but the real  difference is conceptually between China  and the precedents are that the  imbalances have been taken to levels in  China that we've never seen before in  history particularly the consumption  imbalance we've never seen such a low  consumption level and credit is growing  faster than we've ever seen before so  the Chinese exceptionalism probably  suggests a greater rather than a lesser  likelihood of following that pattern  what's the prognosis simple terms if  you've got the a lot of people say you  need a certain level of growth in China  to have you know for it to be socially  stable China has roughly one-fifth of  the world's population and certainly by  most measures it is much less than  one-fifth of almost anything so we're  going to see a lot of that catching up  but i think the economic expectations  for china have been vastly overstated I  don't think China is going to come  anywhere close to achieving some of the  expectation certainly that we had three  or four years ago I think and again the  historical precedent suggests this is  that perceptions are slowly catching up  to the reality but they're going to  continue to lag it for a few more years  the government says seven percent is as  back Tenace it's certain you're saying  that half of that is optimistic yes I  think that if President she is able to  pull off average growth rates of three  to four percent during his ten years in  office he will have accomplished  something that we should really be  astonished that they would it would it  would be truly impressive almost on par  with what done shopping that in the  1980s because he's inherited a much more  difficult economy than we think there  a huge amount of debt there's a huge  amount of unrecognized bad debt and  there is a financial system structured  around a very unbalanced type of  relationship with external volatility  and it's going to be very costly to  adjust but if the adjustment is done  correctly the cost of the adjustment  will be passed on to the what in China  refer to as the vested interests they  benefited disproportionately from 30  years of growth but they will also have  to pay a disproportionate share of the  costs one of the implications is that if  I'm right and Chinese growth does drop  as much as I expected to we will not  have the social disturbances that many  people assume because the growth rate of  ordinary households will drop by much  less in the growth rate of the economy  so three to four percent growth rates  average growth rates for China would be  consistent with five to six percent  growth in household income and  rebalancing by definition means  household income has to grow faster than  gdp a lot of people see things in terms  of markets and obviously that fall in  what happened in Shanghai scared a lot  of people was that significant and was  that something was that in and of itself  a harbinger for future chaos or is that  just something else entirely it it  should have been and among many people  it was fairly fairly well expected not  the specifics but in the in the  adjustment process during the growth  period there is a tendency to structure  balance sheets in such a way that they  exacerbate growth it's basically very  speculative balance sheets and that's a  winning strategy when everything's going  well during the adjustment process that  same sort of balance sheet structure  always leads to great difficulty in  disruption within the financial sector  and China is not proven to be an  exception the key is government  credibility credibility is very high in  China it got it was damaged a little bit